::ENG2_U06_L06_Q01::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] A slide states, "68 of 120 students remain on campus after dismissal." Which oral explanation best develops the point? {
=A. This means more than half of the surveyed students may need a safe place to work after school, so the proposal responds to a documented need. #Correct. The explanation interprets the evidence and connects it to the proposal.
~B. I put this number here because it was in the survey. #Incorrect. This identifies the source but does not explain the meaning.
~C. The number is on the slide, so I will skip it. #Incorrect. Important evidence should be explained orally.
~D. This is a number about school. #Incorrect. This is too vague to develop the point.
}

::ENG2_U06_L06_Q02::[MLA.ENG2.C.02] Which pairing best integrates a slide with speaker notes? {
~A. Slide: a long paragraph. Speaker note: the same paragraph read aloud. #Incorrect. This overloads the slide and creates repetition.
=B. Slide: key survey result. Speaker note: explanation of why the result matters. #Correct. The slide presents the evidence while the speaker note explains its importance.
~C. Slide: decorative picture. Speaker note: unrelated story. #Incorrect. The parts do not support the same message.
~D. Slide: no information. Speaker note: all information. #Incorrect. The digital part should support audience understanding.
}

::ENG2_U06_L06_Q03::[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Which sentence is grammatically correct for a final speaker note? {
~A. The survey results shows a clear need. #Incorrect. The plural subject "results" needs the verb "show."
~B. Because many students remain after school. #Incorrect. This is a fragment because the thought is incomplete.
=C. The survey results show a clear need for supervised study space. #Correct. The subject and verb agree, and the sentence is complete.
~D. The survey results, show a clear need. #Incorrect. The comma incorrectly separates the subject from the verb.
}

::ENG2_U06_L06_Q04::[MLA.ENG2.V.03] Which revision creates the most formal tone? {
~A. "This idea is super great." #Incorrect. This is casual and exaggerated.
~B. "People should obviously agree with us." #Incorrect. This is dismissive rather than reasoned.
~C. "Our slide is pretty cool." #Incorrect. This focuses on appearance in casual language.
=D. "The evidence supports the proposal by showing a specific student need." #Correct. This wording is formal, precise, and evidence-based.
}

::ENG2_U06_L06_Q05::[MLA.ENG2.C.03] Which action best prepares a presentation for submission? {
=A. Review slide clarity, speaker-note alignment, formal tone, and conventions. #Correct. Final communication work should be checked for both content and correctness.
~B. Add extra colors to every slide. #Incorrect. More color does not ensure a stronger presentation.
~C. Remove speaker notes so the slides stand alone. #Incorrect. This lesson requires integrated oral and digital communication.
~D. Make every note informal so it sounds natural. #Incorrect. Academic presentations require an appropriate formal tone.
}

::ENG2_U06_L06_Q06::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] What is the purpose of speaker notes in this lesson? {
~A. To replace all slide content #Incorrect. Speaker notes should work with the slides, not replace them.
=B. To guide oral explanation of the slide evidence and reasoning #Correct. Notes help the speaker explain what the audience should understand.
~C. To list unrelated facts the slide does not mention #Incorrect. Notes should stay aligned with the presentation purpose.
~D. To make the presentation longer even when no explanation is needed #Incorrect. Notes should add meaningful explanation, not unnecessary length.
}

::ENG2_U06_L06_Q07::[MLA.ENG2.C.02] Which slide choice best supports an oral presentation? {
~A. A crowded slide with full paragraphs #Incorrect. Crowded slides make it harder for the audience to follow the speaker.
~B. A slide with unrelated clip art #Incorrect. Unrelated images distract from the message.
=C. A slide with one key result and a clear label #Correct. A focused slide gives the speaker evidence to explain.
~D. A blank slide while the speaker talks #Incorrect. The digital component should support understanding.
}

::ENG2_U06_L06_Q08::[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Which sentence avoids a comma error? {
~A. The proposal, would give students a supervised place to work. #Incorrect. The comma incorrectly separates the subject from the verb.
~B. The proposal would give, students a supervised place to work. #Incorrect. The comma interrupts the verb and object.
~C. The proposal would give students, a supervised place to work. #Incorrect. The comma is not needed between the object and its description here.
=D. The proposal would give students a supervised place to work. #Correct. The sentence is clear and correctly punctuated.
}

::ENG2_U06_L06_Q09::[MLA.ENG2.V.03] Which sentence uses language appropriate for a school presentation? {
=A. The survey evidence suggests that a supervised study space would benefit many students. #Correct. This sentence is formal and evidence-based.
~B. This thing would be awesome for a bunch of people. #Incorrect. This wording is informal and vague.
~C. Everyone should just agree with our idea. #Incorrect. This is demanding rather than academic.
~D. The numbers are kind of cool. #Incorrect. This is casual and does not explain meaning.
}

::ENG2_U06_L06_Q10::[MLA.ENG2.C.03] During a final review, a student sees that a speaker note explains a different claim than the slide. What should the student do? {
~A. Submit it because both parts contain words. #Incorrect. The parts must work together clearly.
=B. Revise the note or slide so they support the same point. #Correct. Alignment between oral and digital parts is required for clear communication.
~C. Delete the slide title only. #Incorrect. This does not solve the mismatch.
~D. Add a brighter background. #Incorrect. Design changes do not fix misaligned content.
}

::ENG2_U06_L06_Q11::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which speaker note best explains a claim instead of repeating it? {
~A. The study space is needed. #Incorrect. This repeats the claim without explanation.
~B. The study space is needed because the study space is needed. #Incorrect. This is circular and does not explain reasoning.
=C. The study space is needed because survey results show many students remain on campus without a supervised academic place to work. #Correct. This explains the reason and uses evidence.
~D. The study space is needed, and that is all. #Incorrect. This does not develop the point.
}

::ENG2_U06_L06_Q12::[MLA.ENG2.C.02] How should the digital slide support the speaker's explanation? {
=A. It should show the key information the speaker will interpret. #Correct. The slide gives the audience a visual anchor for the oral reasoning.
~B. It should include unrelated images to hold attention. #Incorrect. Attention should be connected to the message.
~C. It should hide the evidence until the end. #Incorrect. Evidence should be visible when it is being discussed.
~D. It should use tiny text so more words fit. #Incorrect. Tiny text weakens readability.
}

::ENG2_U06_L06_Q13::[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Which revision fixes the error in this sentence: "Students remains on campus after dismissal"? {
=A. Students remain on campus after dismissal. #Correct. The plural subject "students" takes the verb "remain."
~B. Students remaining on campus after dismissal. #Incorrect. This is a fragment, not a complete sentence.
~C. Students remains, on campus after dismissal. #Incorrect. This keeps the subject-verb error and adds an unnecessary comma.
~D. Student remain on campus after dismissal. #Incorrect. This creates a singular subject with a plural verb.
}

::ENG2_U06_L06_Q14::[MLA.ENG2.V.03] Which speaker note should be revised for tone? {
~A. The evidence indicates that the need affects many students. #Incorrect. This sentence is formal and appropriate.
=B. This totally proves our plan is the best ever. #Correct. This should be revised because it is exaggerated and informal.
~C. The survey result supports the proposal. #Incorrect. This is formal and clear.
~D. The data helps explain why the solution matters. #Incorrect. This is appropriate academic language.
}

::ENG2_U06_L06_Q15::[MLA.ENG2.C.03] Which final checklist item best supports publication-ready communication? {
~A. Check only the first slide because it is most visible. #Incorrect. Every part of the presentation should be reviewed.
~B. Check whether the slides are decorative enough. #Incorrect. Decoration is not the main standard for readiness.
=C. Check that each slide and note are clear, aligned, correct, and formal. #Correct. This addresses clarity, integration, conventions, and tone.
~D. Check whether the presentation uses the maximum number of words. #Incorrect. More words do not guarantee quality.
}

::ENG2_U06_L06_Q16::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which oral delivery choice best supports audience understanding? {
~A. Read every slide word for word without explanation. #Incorrect. Reading alone does not develop the reasoning.
=B. Explain how the evidence supports the proposal. #Correct. Oral delivery should help the audience understand the connection between evidence and claim.
~C. Skip all evidence because it is on the slide. #Incorrect. Evidence should be interpreted orally.
~D. Speak informally so the presentation feels casual. #Incorrect. Academic presentations require appropriate tone.
}

::ENG2_U06_L06_Q17::[MLA.ENG2.C.02] Which slide and note combination is weakest? {
~A. Slide: "Survey Evidence." Note: "The survey result supports the proposal." #Incorrect. This is more focused than the vague option.
~B. Slide: "68 of 120 students remain after school." Note: "This shows a need that affects many students." #Incorrect. This combination clearly pairs evidence with explanation.
~C. Slide: "Proposal: supervised study space." Note: "The solution responds to the documented need." #Incorrect. This is aligned and clear.
=D. Slide: "Need." Note: "This is important and stuff." #Correct. Both parts are vague and do not communicate clear evidence or reasoning.
}

::ENG2_U06_L06_Q18::[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Which sentence is a complete sentence? {
~A. Because the evidence supports the proposal. #Incorrect. This is a dependent clause and needs a complete thought.
=B. The evidence supports the proposal because it shows a student need. #Correct. This is a complete sentence with a clear reason.
~C. Showing a student need after school. #Incorrect. This is a fragment.
~D. The evidence, because it shows a student need. #Incorrect. This is not a complete sentence.
}

::ENG2_U06_L06_Q19::[MLA.ENG2.V.03] Which word choice is most precise for a formal explanation? {
~A. thing #Incorrect. This word is vague.
~B. stuff #Incorrect. This word is informal and imprecise.
=C. proposal #Correct. This word precisely names the plan being presented.
~D. whatever #Incorrect. This word is informal and unclear.
}

::ENG2_U06_L06_Q20::[MLA.ENG2.C.03] A student's slide is clear, but the speaker note has grammar errors. What should happen before submission? {
~A. Submit it because the slide is clear. #Incorrect. The oral script also affects final communication quality.
~B. Delete the speaker note and submit only the slide. #Incorrect. The assignment requires integrated oral and digital communication.
~C. Add a new image to cover the errors. #Incorrect. Images do not correct language errors.
=D. Revise the speaker note for correct conventions. #Correct. Publication-ready work requires clear and correct language.
}

::ENG2_U06_L06_Q21::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which note best shows modeled reasoning? {
=A. Since 68 of 120 surveyed students remain on campus, the problem affects a large portion of respondents. #Correct. This note interprets the number and explains why it matters.
~B. The survey happened. #Incorrect. This does not explain the result.
~C. There are students and a number. #Incorrect. This is vague and does not show reasoning.
~D. This slide is about school. #Incorrect. This is too general to help the audience.
}

::ENG2_U06_L06_Q22::[MLA.ENG2.C.02] Why should a slide not contain the full speaker script? {
~A. The audience should not see any information. #Incorrect. The audience should see key information.
=B. A full script can overload the slide and make the speaker unnecessary. #Correct. Slides should highlight key information while the speaker explains it.
~C. Speaker notes are never useful. #Incorrect. Speaker notes are useful when they guide explanation.
~D. Digital presentations should avoid evidence. #Incorrect. Digital presentations should include clear evidence.
}

::ENG2_U06_L06_Q23::[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Which sentence correctly uses a comma after an introductory phrase? {
=A. After dismissal, many students remain on campus. #Correct. The comma correctly follows the introductory phrase.
~B. After dismissal many students, remain on campus. #Incorrect. The comma is missing after the introductory phrase and incorrectly placed later.
~C. After, dismissal many students remain on campus. #Incorrect. The comma incorrectly splits the phrase.
~D. After dismissal many students remain, on campus. #Incorrect. The comma placement is incorrect.
}

::ENG2_U06_L06_Q24::[MLA.ENG2.V.03] Which revision changes casual language into formal language? {
~A. "Clear need" to "kind of something." #Incorrect. This weakens clarity and tone.
~B. "Evidence supports" to "stuff says." #Incorrect. This makes the wording less formal.
~C. "Proposal" to "thing." #Incorrect. This makes the wording vague.
=D. "Kids hang around" to "students remain on campus." #Correct. The revision is more formal and precise.
}

::ENG2_U06_L06_Q25::[MLA.ENG2.C.03] Which statement best describes a ready-to-submit integrated presentation? {
=A. The slides present key evidence clearly, and the speaker notes explain that evidence in formal, correct language. #Correct. This describes aligned digital and oral communication that is ready for submission.
~B. The slides look interesting, but the notes are unrelated. #Incorrect. The parts must be aligned.
~C. The notes are long, but the slides hide the evidence. #Incorrect. Evidence should be visible and explained.
~D. The presentation has many colors, but no clear reasoning. #Incorrect. Visual style cannot replace reasoning.
}
